const e = practitionerB5.find(p => p.slug === 'streaming-first-token-finished-answer')!;
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="Our fictional Route A starts after 0.2 seconds but finishes after about 20.2. Route B starts after a second and finishes after about nine. Which feels faster depends on what the user needs.">

<p>A chat window begins printing quickly. A document extractor finishes quickly. Those are different experiences and need different measurements. If your tracker keeps only a tokens-per-second figure, it cannot show how long somebody waited before useful work began or how long the entire job took.</p>
<p>The <a href={e.sources[0].url}>Claude streaming guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes incremental events, content blocks and a final message-stop event. We suggest recording several timestamps around that flow, with names that say what actually happened.</p>
<h2>Choose the event that the user can use</h2>
<p>Record request submission, first useful answer text and successful completion after your application checks the result. You can also record the first network event, but keep it separate. An acknowledgement or a non-text block is not necessarily the start of the answer the person is waiting to read.</p>
<p>For an extractor, the useful event may be a complete validated object. For a long explanation, readable partial text may help sooner. Choose the event from the job rather than from whichever timestamp is easiest for an SDK to expose.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Fictional route","Start delay, seconds","Emission, tokens/second","Approximate finish, seconds"]} rows={examples['streaming-first-token-finished-answer']} caption="Idealised 800-token output at constant emission rates. This is arithmetic, not a measured service comparison." />
<p>The table assumes a constant emission rate for 800 tokens after a start delay. The finish estimate is delay plus tokens divided by rate. It is a continuous approximation that ignores the spacing of individual tokens. Neither route is a real provider, and the example says nothing about answer quality.</p>
<h2>Keep output length with the timing</h2>
<p>Compare like workloads. A short answer can finish earlier while being emitted more slowly. A model that writes an unnecessary essay may show impressive output speed and still make the user wait longer for the result. Record the actual output length and whether the answer was accepted.</p>
<p>If you impose a length limit, keep that setting with the run. A request that stops at its limit before completing the job belongs in the failure record. It should not win a latency comparison merely because it ended early.</p>
<p>For tool workflows, separate time spent in external operations from time spent waiting on model responses where your instrumentation permits it. The user experiences the whole delay. The split helps diagnose it, but it should not replace the total in a product-facing comparison.</p>
<h2>Report a distribution and the missing results</h2>
<p>Keep per-request timings, then state how you calculate the median and any upper percentile. Specify whether failures and timeouts are included or shown separately. Dropping the slowest unfinished requests can make the surviving set look better while the service gets less useful.</p>
<p>Use the same stop condition for each candidate. If one application declares success when the last token arrives and another waits for validation, their finish times describe different work. Save both timestamps if you want to compare generation and application completion.</p>
<p>Repeat at a realistic arrival pattern. A quiet sequential trial establishes behaviour under that trial's conditions. It does not establish what happens when a queue forms. Record concurrency, request sizes and the time of the observation so the comparison can be repeated.</p>
<h2>Use the result to choose a route for a job</h2>
<p>For interactive prose, inspect the start of useful text and whether partial answers remain readable. For jobs consumed by software, inspect accepted completion and the tail of the timing distribution. In both cases keep price and acceptance beside the timing rather than combining them into an unexplained speed score.</p>
<p>A useful tracker can say that a route starts sooner, another completes sooner, and the evidence comes from a specified workload. That is more informative than declaring one model fast without saying what finished.</p>
<PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
